  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and perhaps a thunderstorm are possible over the  \nsouthern tier of PA this afternoon and this evening. Dry \nconditions for Friday before unsettled weather returns for the  \nweekend.\n\nSfc cold front was located from near KELM and KELZ WSW to around\nKBFD and KGKJ at 16Z and drifting slowly SEWD.\n\nSlight veering of the low-mid level flow from west to the west-\nnorthwest throughout the day today will help to keep the bulk \nof any showers (and possibly a low-topped TSRA) confined to the \nsouthern tier counties of Penn this afternoon and evening with\nsfc dewpoints in the mid 60s to near 70F.\n\nLarger scale, more subtle forcing beneath the right entrance \nregion of a 130 kt upper jet stretched from Lake Huron to the \nMiddle St Lawrence River Valley will help to produce a few light\nshowers across the NW and Ncent Mtns of PA this afternoon and\nearly evening, but QPF will be limited to a few or svrl \nhundredths of an inch in most spots up north.\n\nThe aforementioned surface cold front won't likely push through\nthe southern third of the CWA until this evening, so there will\nbe additional SHRA today with up to 60 pct in Somerset Co. A \nstray shower (no thunder) could also affect Warren/McKean Cos as\nthe cool air aloft helps create some very weak instability \nalong the NY border and the cold front passes. However, \ninstability over the CWA will be low (<500J/kg in the far SW), \nso only some isolated thunder is expected - and only in the \nLaurels. The risk for heavy rain has decreased markedly through \nthe last 48 hrs of model runs, and the WPC ERO MRGL risk area \nwon't touch PA anymore (stays to our S) with the new guidance to\nbe issued soon. The most-possible time for rain will be the \nafternoon and early evening as some of the weak convection moves\nin from the SW. The dewpoints don't come down until the front \narrives (mid- late morning N, close to sunset in the S). So, it \nmay still feel a little muggy today, esp from I-80 southward \nwith 65-70F Tds all day long. \n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand mainly sunny Friday. AM valley fog is almost a given. The\nextent of the fog is a question, but we'll paint it in all the\nnormal nrn valleys at this point. Low level moisture lurking \njust south of the Mason/Dixon line Fri night will advance NE \nacross the state on Saturday as SFC high pressure moves well off\nthe Mid Atlantic and Southern New England Coast. This will lead\nto some showers spreading NE across the region with a few PM \nTSRA possible Saturday and/or Sunday afternoons, especially over\nthe western mtns. A more-active (wetter) pattern is in store \nfor Mon-Tues. But, the flat upper ridge over the southern half \nof the CONUS will make it difficult for deep moisture to make it\ninto PA ahead of any shots of atmos forcing. The week could \nend dry as the upper ridge pumps up over the central CONUS.",
